<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Royal Titles Act 1876</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Royal Titles Act 1876 was a law of the British Parliament that allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> to take the title Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Promoted by the prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-disraeli">Benjamin Disraeli</a>, it was opposed by many Liberals as un-English. The new title was proclaimed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-durbar">Delhi Durbar</a> of 1 January 1877 and was used by British monarchs until shortly after the independence of India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1947.</p></div>
